#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843|author=w:Thomas Carlyle|title=[[:w:Past and Present (book)|Past and Present]]|section=book 2, chapter 6, ''Monk Samson''|passage=Once, slipping the money clandestinely, just in the act of taking leave, he slipt it not into her hand but on the floor, and another had it; whereupon the poor Monk, coming to know it, '''looked''' mere despair for some days{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|often|with "for"}} To [[search]] for, to [[try]] to [[find]].
# To face or present a view.
#: {{ux|en|The hotel '''looks''' over the valleys of the Hindu Kush.}}
#* '''1769''', {{w|Benjamin Blayney}} (editor), ''{{w|King James Version|King James Bible}}'', Oxford standard text, ''[[s:Bible (King James)/Ezekiel|Ezekiel]]'', xi, 1,
#*: Moreover the spirit lifted me up, and brought me unto the east gate of the LORD's house, which '''looketh''' eastward:
#* {{RQ:Dunsany Pegana
|passage=Wornath-Mavai lieth in a valley and '''looketh''' towards the south, and on the slopes of it Sish rested among the flowers when Sish was young.}}
# To expect or anticipate.
#: {{ux|en|I '''look''' to each hour for my lover’s arrival.}}
#* '''1596''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''{{w|The Fairie Queene}}'', Book VI, Canto XI, '''1750''', ''The Works of Edmund Spenser'', Volume 4, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=PyxMAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA139&dq=%22looking+each+hour+into+death%27s+mouth+to+fall%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjTjumR2vLZAhVSOZAKHTDnBAMQ6AEIJzAA#v=onepage&q=%22looking%20each%20hour%20into%20death's%20mouth%20to%20fall%22&f=false page 139],
#* {{RQ:Noire Thug-A-Licious|page=108|passage="Ain't gone be no Rikers Island for you next time," I warned him. "You get tapped on another gun charge and you '''looking''' at some upstate time."<br>'''Looking''' each Hour into Death's Mouth to fall,}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|1|1|passage=Gloster, what ere we like, thou art Protector,<br>And '''lookest''' to command the Prince and Realme.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[express]] or [[manifest]] by a look.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1815|author=w:Lord Byron|title=Waterloo
|passage=Soft eyes '''looked''' love to eyes which spake again,}}
#* {{RQ:Bramah Eyes of Max Carrados|passage=For a few seconds Mr Carlyle '''looked''' his unutterable feelings. When he did speak it was with crushing deliberation.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|often|with "to"}} To make sure of, to see to.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1898|translator=w:Samuel Butler|author=w:Homer|title=[[s:The Odyssey (Butler)/Book XXIII|The Odyssey]]
|passage="'''Look''' to it yourself, father," answered Telemachus, "for they say you are the wisest counsellor in the world, and that there is no other mortal man who can compare with you.{{nb...}}}}
# {{lb|en|dated|sometimes|figurative}} To show oneself in looking; to [[peep out]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|Induction|2|text=I have{{...}}more feet than shoes, or such shoes as my toes '''look''' through the overleather.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic|_|or|_|dialectal}} To [[check#Verb|check]], to [[make sure#Verb|make sure]] (of something).
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=II|page=151|passage=Finding it was like to overblow, we took in our Sprit-ſail, and ſtood by to hand the Fore-ſail; but making foul Weather, we '''look'd''' the Guns were all faſt, and handed the Miſſen.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To look at; to turn the eyes toward.
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I|text=Serene, smiling, enigmatic, she faced him with no fear whatever showing in her dark eyes.{{...}}She put back a truant curl from her forehead where it had sought egress to the world, and '''looked''' him full in the face now,{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[seek]]; to search for.
#* {{circa|1552–1599}}, {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, unidentified sonnet,
#*: '''Looking''' my love, I go from place to place, <br> Like a young fawn that late hath lost the hind; <br> And seek each where, where last I saw her face, <br> Whose image yet I carry fresh in mind.
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To influence, overawe, or subdue by looks or presence.
#: {{ux|en|to '''look''' down opposition}}
#* '''1692''', {{w|John Dryden}}, ''Cleomenes the Spartan Hero, a Tragedy'', Act 3, Scene 1, '''1701''', ''The Comedies, Tragedies, and Operas Written by John Dryden, Esq'', Volume 2, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=zehWAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA1-PA464&dq=%22A+spirit+fit+to+start+into+an+empire,+And+look+the+world+to+law.%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiwjsH0nvPZAhWHqJQKHacPACsQ6AEITTAH#v=onepage&q=%22A%20spirit%20fit%20to%20start%20into%20an%20empire%2C%20And%20look%20the%20world%20to%20law.%22&f=false page 464],
#*: A Spirit fit to start into an Empire, <br> And '''look''' the World to Law.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1882|author=w:Wilkie Collins|title=Heart and Science
|passage=Ovid might have evaded her entreaties by means of an excuse. But her eyes were irresistible: they '''looked''' him into submission in an instant.}}
# {{senseid|en|baseball}}{{lb|en|baseball}} To [[look at]] a pitch as a batter without swinging at it.
#: {{ux|en|The fastball caught him '''looking.'''}}
#: {{ux|en|Clem Labine struck Mays out '''looking''' at his last at bat.}}
